{"type": "vertex_text", "round": 1, "text": "I have completed a full source-fidelity review of the entire patent application draft, including all claims, the detailed description, the abstract, the numeral list, and all figure briefs. I traced every numbered part, every claim limitation, and every described structure, relationship, and function back to the affirmative inventor-provided source materials in `input/disclosure.md` and `input/conversation.md`. The draft is largely faithful to the source and internally consistent, but I found two instances where figure briefs describe a condition that is not depicted in the corresponding drawing, which is an internal inconsistency that must be corrected before filing.\n\nMy review confirmed that the claims are fully supported by the disclosure, all numbered parts are defined and used consistently, and the technical descriptions in the summary and detailed description accurately reflect the inventor's source text. The use of \"shown schematically\" in the figure briefs for generic shapes like rectangular bodies is acceptable as a depiction convention because the claims and description remain shape-neutral and the chosen geometry is a minimal representation necessary to illustrate the disclosed function.\n\nThe two findings relate to descriptions in the figure briefs for FIG. 1 and FIG. 5 that refer to an assembled or disassembled state, whereas the figures themselves depict an exploded view and an assembled view, respectively. These descriptions must be revised to only describe what is actually visible in the drawing to maintain internal consistency. The provided fixes correct these inconsistencies without altering the substantive disclosure."}
